# Jido Instances

A Jido instance owns the local Registry, task supervisor, Agent supervisor, and
runtime coordination store. This Livebook starts a named instance.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Define An Application Instance

```elixir
defmodule InstanceGuide.Jido do
  use Jido, otp_app: :jido
end
```

In an application, add `InstanceGuide.Jido` to the application supervision
tree before any component that starts Agents.

## Start The Instance

```elixir
{:ok, instance} = InstanceGuide.Jido.start_link([])

%{
  registry: InstanceGuide.Jido.registry_name(),
  agent_supervisor: InstanceGuide.Jido.agent_supervisor_name(),
  task_supervisor: InstanceGuide.Jido.task_supervisor_name(),
  runtime_store: InstanceGuide.Jido.runtime_store_name()
}
```

Use a fixed application module or another finite atom as the instance name. Do
not create atoms from external tenant IDs.

## Define And Start An Agent

```elixir
defmodule InstanceGuide.Agent do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "instance_guide_agent"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)})
  end
end

{:ok, agent} =
  InstanceGuide.Jido.start_agent(
    InstanceGuide.Agent,
    id: "agent-1"
  )

InstanceGuide.Jido.list_agents()
```

The generated instance functions call the same public top-level Jido API with
the instance name already supplied.

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = InstanceGuide.Jido.stop_agent(agent)
:ok = Supervisor.stop(instance)
```

The runtime store is local and ephemeral. Use configured persistence when
Agent state must survive a complete instance or VM loss.

Continue with [Start And Address Agents](start-and-address-agents.livemd) and
[Configuration](configuration.md).
